{
 "_notes": "three.js conventions: Y up, right-handed, metres, ground plane y=0. Camera at (0,h,0) with the stairs along -Z; stair centre line x = 0.131. camera.rotationEuler = [x,y,z] radians, order 'YXZ' (yaw about Y, pitch about X, roll 0); lookAt is an equivalent target. fovVertical in degrees (three.js PerspectiveCamera.fov), derived from a 35 mm lens on 36 mm sensor width (horizontal fov) at 16:9. Proxy box: center, size [x,y,z] full extents, rotationY radians. Cylinder: center (middle), radius, height. plate_03: head-on stairway (13 steps) with side walls and handrails; camera at (0,h,0), the stairs recede along -Z, centred at x 0.13. Camera by tools/stair_fit.py: the receding lines (handrail tubes, wall coping) follow the stair slope, so their VP is the slope VP; pitch, camera height, rise and the depth of the first riser come from a fit of 13 traced nosing rows + 13 riser-foot rows + the slope VP row, with the stair-proportion prior 2*rise + run = 0.63 m (Blondel), which also sets the scale; the fitted horizon (row 247) agrees with the pavement joints (about row 250); the painted handrail then stands only about 0.52 m above the nosing line (not 0.9 m: the painting is not self-consistent); yaw from the slope VP. proxies: step_NN = box per step (riser + tread, y from 0 to NN*rise, one run deep); landing = box at y1; post_left/right = newel posts at the foot; wall_left_NN/wall_right_NN = stepped side walls (wall top above the nosing line from the post height); flank_left/right = the big wall fronts beside the stairs; floor = ground. Walkable height (optional): walkable.surfaces = [{name, polygon [[x,z],...], y} | {name, polygon, stairs:{bottom:[[x,z],[x,z]], top:[[x,z],[x,z]], y0, y1, steps, rise, run}}]; surfaces do not overlap in plan and together cover walkable.outer minus holes; flat surface height = y; on stairs the body height is a continuous ramp from y0 at bottom to y1 at top, a foot stands on the tread top y0 + rise*(index+1), index = floor(distance from bottom / run); stairs.polygon = bottom-left, bottom-right, top-right, top-left; bottom/top = the stair's bottom and top edges (left to right); y1 = y0 + steps*rise. The 13th tread (last run before the top edge) is already at landing height y1. A map without surfaces is all y = 0. arrivals.position stays [x,z]; the app takes y from the surface. Painting caveat: the landing is painted as a surface seen from above (rows 131-166) but the fitted camera (0.93 m) is below the landing level (1.46 m), so the model landing top spans rows 170-190+ below the top nosing and a character on the landing is drawn over the top steps; the landing is only 2 m deep. The ground in front of the stairs is a short strip (about 1.3 m to the bottom of the view, which the walkable area extends 0.6 m beyond: viewMarginBottom -0.6). No plate masks: no occluding silhouette is non-box. Hand-read pixel picks in tools/maps/plate_03.json. exits: [{name, zone: polygon [[x,z],...] on the ground inside the walkable area, to: target map id, arrival: arrival name in the target map}]; the character entering a zone leaves the map. arrivals: [{name, position: [x,z], facingY}] spawn points; facingY radians about Y, model forward +Z (glTF), same convention as markers (0 = faces +Z, toward the camera on a street map; pi = faces -Z). Arrival 'start' is the default spawn. Every arrival is inside the walkable area, outside every exit zone, at least 0.5 m from zones, holes and the walkable edge (checked by build_scene.py).",
